/** siman 442 part 2 — ta'aruvot chametz */
export const fixes = {
  "output/siman_442/beur-hagra/part-001.txt": {
    "1:א": "Seif 1 — chametz mixture, etc. All four opening seifim are the language of Rambam chapter 4.",
    "1:ב": "They transgress regarding it. Per Rashi's explanation and likewise the Geonim; Maggid Mishneh wrote only when there is a kezayit within k'dei achilat peras — not so, since there is no lav in its eating as he wrote chapter 1 — likewise bal yera'eh. Some Geonim hold even with less than this one transgresses; likewise Ramach explained our Rabbi's words but disagrees with him — see Maggid Mishneh chapter 4. Only chametz in which there is a kezayit within k'dei achilat peras, etc.; but Ramach's words are primary since he cited Babylonian paste and Median beer of which the Gemara says they lack k'dei achilat peras. Also since he distinguished in chapter 1 regarding eating and here did not distinguish — learn that in all cases one is liable; likewise Kesef Mishneh. What Maggid Mishneh compared to eating — Rambam does not hold so, for also regarding chametz nokshah he wrote chapter 4 clothes they laundered, etc., and papers glued — one transgresses bal yera'eh and bal yimatzei because the form of chametz does not stand — implying if not so one transgresses even though they are chametz nokshah as the mishnah says kolan shel sofrim, etc. — see Maggid Mishneh there and Ra'avad there in hasagot.",
    "1:ג": "Such as morayis. See Rashi Avodah Zarah 29b s.v. hamorayis; see Rosh Kol Sha'ah seif 1 and chapter 2 daf 19 16b — it implies they put flour into it.",
    "1:ד": "And paste, etc. The mishnah.",
    "1:ה": "And all the like. There.",
    "1:ו": "And theriac. Likewise R' Hillel the Gaon learned from the above and Rosh brought it. He wrote all the more so from melogma.",
    "2:א": "Seif 2 — bread, etc., and it was disqualified — there 45b: Tanna: bread, etc.; Rif wrote: if it was disqualified from being eaten by a dog one need not burn it, as written \"like whom,\" etc. — they stated a general rule, etc. — see Tosafos 21b s.v. charco.",
    "2:ב": "And melogma. Tosefta chapter 3; and even though in Yerushalmi chapter 2 halachah 6 only when it putrefied and afterward became chametz — there it deals with putrefaction within Pesach. Nevertheless one must say so, for the kneading-trough of the tanners to which he did not put hides and bread that molded — all became chametz before, only within Pesach; and as written in seif 9 we require specifically before its time — see there. But Ra'avad, Rosh, and Tur hold even before Pesach only when it putrefied and afterward became chametz. Tur wrote: what they did not distinguish in Tosefta regarding kilyor, ritya, and aspalnith — also because they immediately putrefy.",
    "3:א": "Seif 3 — clothes, etc. He learned from kilyorot that one may retain them even though absorbed — this is the view of those who are stringent in the gloss; but the first view in the gloss holds as Maggid Mishneh who learned from the kneading-trough to which he put hides; likewise Rambam's language that there is no form, etc., and as in seif 1 regarding kilyor for it is spoiled, etc., and there the chametz is b'eyna.",
    "3:ב": "And likewise. Like kilyor, etc.; and it appears for kolan shel sofrim there it deals before the papers were glued. Nevertheless.",
    "3:ג": "And therefore. From Maggid Mishneh's words that he learned from the tanners' kneading-trough, etc.",
    "3:ד": "And there are those who are stringent. And so it implies from Tur in the name of Ravyah who wrote the reason because then it is recognizable; therefore he explains Rambam's words regarding what is not visible — see Terumat HaDeshen; see Beit Yosef; see Magen Avraham.",
    "4:א": "Seif 4 — a thing, etc. Since it says there and there he is not obligated to destroy — it implies forbidden in eating there, etc., and permitted in benefit — plainly no need that it was charred, etc.; but in eating forbidden.",
    "4:ב": "And even though there is not. He holds that even though it was nullified before Pesach in sixty, on Pesach it revives in any amount; likewise many Geonim. Nevertheless see siman 447 seif 4.",
    "5:א": "Seif 5 — and beer they make. For even Median beer into which barley is thrown one must destroy as in seif 1 — all the more this whose essence is barley; and even the Rabbis concede here he is liable as written mishnah 1:1 that the Rabbis concede regarding a kezayit within k'dei achilat peras; and even per Rabbenu Tam who explains the mishnah \"they transgress from the table\" but is not obligated to destroy — he concedes here. Rosh.",
    "5:ב": "And likewise if. Meaning even per Rabbenu Tam as above because here it is b'eyna and not a mixture as in Avodah Zarah 35a — here since he establishes it, etc. And all this the Gemara teaches per Rabbenu Tam's view.",
    "6:א": "Seif 6 — they practiced. He relied on this in Yerushalmi chapter 2 halachah 2: Rav said one who coats his house with dough is obligated to destroy. Ra'avan seif 7.",
    "6:ב": "And if there is chametz. As in the Gemara 45b: if he coated, etc. — see there, Mordechai, Tur; likewise Yerushalmi there on Rav's words: obligated to destroy. Tanna: Rashba — dough he made as a patch is nullified — explain it thus, or they disagree with Rashba, or the patch is repulsive and meaning he spread in mud as in the Gemara — likewise Ra'avan; see Mordechai.",
    "7:א": "Seif 7 — dough, etc. Like Rav Huna there who said: remove my kilyorot, etc. — Abaye's explanation is not the halachah for he establishes like Rashba and they said there the halachah is not like Rashba; Rav Ashi comes to explain Abaye's matter and not to fix halachah — see Razah and Sefer HaMilchamot.",
    "7:ב": "Or to seal. Per R' Chananel and Rishonim who explained asuy lechazek means dough that filled up; R' Chananel explains like Abaye; likewise Rashi and Tosafos; see Tur; see Sefer HaMilchamot — no distinction between inside and outside, threshold, or below; only Abaye outside we rule like the first version and inside where there is concern it will stick to kneading we are stringent like the latter version; likewise Tosafos s.v. kan, etc. Razah's method like Rashi: three distinctions — ledges, walls, and upper lip; per Rashi not in a kneading place is stricter — the upper lip; likewise walls outside as in the Gemara there — except Razah and Sefer HaMilchamot: even though he explains like Abaye, nevertheless regarding even a kezayit not obligated to destroy we do not rule like Rashba as in patch — the halachah is not like Rashba; four views emerge: Rashi, Tosafos, Razah, Ramban; Rif and Rambam like R' Hillel — no distinction at all between places; and a sixth view — R' Yechiel of Paris explains Rashi as in seif 11.",
    "8:א": "Seif 8 — in what case in a kneading-trough. Magen Avraham s.k. 1.",
    "8:ב": "Even though if. It appears even without any thread at all as Rashi wrote there; likewise in the Gemara since they asked regarding a house, attic, and two houses, etc.",
    "8:ג": "Since, etc. He wrote thus to resolve Tosafos' difficulties s.v. kan — per Tosafos not in a kneading place there is no concern of mixing even if not asuy lechazek — less than a kezayit is nullified; likewise a house — not so Rambam; therefore he wrote since, etc., and it is as asuy lechazek; see Milchamot who wrote since it is stuck he is not particular, etc. — see there.",
    "8:ד": "One nullifies it. For a rabbinic doubt is lenient.",
    "9:א": "Seif 9 — chametz as above seif 2; Ra'avad's view that se'or always requires destruction as Yoma 7b; but patch of se'or Ra'avad emends to bread of se'or, etc., and the halachah is not like Rashba. All this may be rejected: it deals where it was not disqualified from a dog; but in Tosefta and the Gemara brought there — se'or from others' chametz, chametz leavened from others — from when is it called se'or? From when it is disqualified from a dog — see there Ra'avad.",
    "9:ב": "Before its time, etc., or they burned, etc., before. 21b: charco before its time, etc.",
    "9:ג": "And charred until. Tosafos there; they learned from moldy bread as above; there 45b moldy bread is obligated to destroy, etc., such as not disqualified from a dog. Rosh there.",
    "9:ד": "Or, etc., and coat. Gemara there — not like Rashba.",
    "10:_": "Seif 10 — ink, etc. Like charco before its time permitted in benefit after its time; and it appears regarding ink from mei einayim forbidden in benefit — there like charco after its time; and like melogma within Pesach that became chametz and afterward putrefied forbidden to retain as above; and even though he wrote in the name of Tosafos Avodah Zarah chapter 2 — not only per those who forbid stam yeinam in benefit is ink forbidden, but even per Rabbenu Tam who permits stam yeinam in benefit ink is forbidden lest he put the quill in his mouth; likewise here — nevertheless forbidden in eating as in seif 4; nevertheless the prohibition is only since he intends to eat it he designates it for eating, and for him it is not disqualified even from a person; and Rosh beginning chapter 21 — all the more not so here without intent; see Yoreh De'ah siman 133 seif 13.",
    "11:א": "Seif 11 — and the vessel combines them. R' Yitzchak's language distinguishes, per his method in Hagahot Semak following Rashi: not in a kneading place such as the upper lip outside — even less than a kezayit one must destroy, meaning there are many olive-fractions and the vessel combines them even without joining with a thread; the requirement of a thread is in the walls for the vessel does not combine on its outside walls as below — thereby Tosafos' difficulty s.v. kan is resolved: a house, although similar to the upper lip, nevertheless a house does not combine like a vessel and one needs the reason sometimes it compresses — end of his words. Likewise in the mishnah: if there is a kezayit in one place, less than this, etc. — implies not in one place; on this they said \"if not,\" etc.; likewise Yerushalmi there — R' Yirmiyah in the name of R' Zeira: two half-olives in a house — the house does not combine; in a vessel the vessel combines — only inside, not outside on walls as above — it follows the Gemara's sugya except the Gemara concerned compression; one may resolve Yerushalmi also concerns compression but compression applies only to moist dough that sticks together and becomes one kezayit — in that case two half-olives of the Gemara; if one removed the thread it is removed with it — only when moist as Yerushalmi; Yerushalmi does not deal with moist dough where scraping does not help, as on the mishnah kezayit in one place, etc., peeled all at once; and even though they said there while moist, when dry — since if moist it would peel all at once, there it is already stuck as one except if peeled it would scatter — then what is written in chapter 1 \"if we say because of crumbs,\" etc., fits; per Rambam whatever is not stuck to walls, etc. — even less one must destroy and not nullified; per this view it fits that only because of combination the house does not combine, and the gezeirah of compression does not apply to something dry — not like Rambam who requires sticking; likewise Yerushalmi there challenged on \"within the vessel the vessel combines\" — but we taught less than this is nullified in minority — proof for R' Yitzchak's explanation that the mishnah deals where there are many olive-fractions as above; they answer: here detached, here attached — mishnah attached, Yerushalmi detached — thus even detached in a house no need to destroy without compression; Yerushalmi did not descend to distinguish asuy lechazek or not, kneading place or not — they mentioned nothing of this; nevertheless one may resolve Yerushalmi's sugya per our sugya: what they distinguished between detached and attached per Rashi is in the walls, like two half-olives and thread, etc., in the walls as above; detached in all cases the vessel combines but the house never combines. Likewise Tosafos' method which all Rishonim accepted — one may explain per R' Yitzchak even though Tosefta does not imply so; for halachah one explains their method per Yerushalmi: in a kneading place and not asuy lechazek even less than a kezayit one must destroy — many olive-fractions; likewise Rif everywhere not asuy lechazek also many olive-fractions; no need to force that two half-olives in a house deals with stuck ones as asuy lechazek; also crumbs cannot be resolved except per R' Yitzchak — primary per Rif; Rashba is lone against Rif and R' Yitzchak.",
    "11:ב": "And one must give them. As in siman 468 seif 3 from Tosefta and Magen Avraham.",
    "11:ג": "Or coat with clay. As above from patch as in seif 10 and seif 9.",
    "11:ד": "And likewise for dough. Per Rashi only on the upper lip outside; per Rif everywhere since not asuy lechazek; likewise Tosafos since in a kneading place and not asuy lechazek.",
    "11:ה": "And from a cloth that was, etc. See end Pesachim 7a 7b: these leniencies, etc.; see Tosafos there s.v. dekisna, etc.; Magen Avraham s.k. 19 wrote: it appears the dough stuck, etc.; and even though Tosafos did not write to distinguish stuck except in something hot — see Rosh and Hagahot Ashri; nevertheless from Zevachim 79b 40 it applies even in cold use; see Rashi s.v. ve'afilu and s.v. she'ani."
  },
  "output/siman_442/biur-halacha/part-001.txt": {
    "3:_": "And there are those who are stringent if visible from outside — from Acharonim' conclusion it appears we adopt l'chatchila the view of those who are stringent except Peri Chadash who wrote the primary view is the first view [and from Taz it also implies for practical halachah the primary view is the first] that there is no concern if visible from outside since it dried and thereby is disqualified from a dog's eating — see there; regarding laundering clothes this is also explained in Tur in the name of Rishonim to permit for this reason; since he included there also the law of glued papers it implies all one reason as Peri Chadash. Bookbinders who glue papers with chametz glue even before Pesach — per the first view certainly permitted for it dries immediately as above; per those who are stringent one may also say permitted for chametz is not recognizable outside; what Terumat HaDeshen wrote per those stringent to make it soft at night — one may say only for windows so it will be absorbed between cracks and not protrude outside; especially per Kaf HaChayim since its essence is chametz nokshah it is forbidden only when seen outside and he did not mention the condition it be soft at night; likewise Chayei Adam did not mention this condition — see there. Apart from all this one may say our matter is unlike windows where ultimately one will scrape chametz from windows; likewise glued papers one may separate — not so bookbinders covered outside and made l'chatchila to remain so — possibly it is in the category of chametz that fell on it from collapse as explained.",
    "9:א": "Chametz that molded, etc., and was disqualified from a dog's eating — know Ra'avad's view that for se'or one must destroy even if it molded until disqualified from a dog; per Rambam no distinction — see Maggid Mishneh; Acharonim ruled halachah like Rambam; Magen Avraham cited; see Peri Chadash and Gra who brought support for Ra'avad from Tosefta Beitzah chapter 1 that se'or is called from when disqualified from a dog; Peri Chadash ruled from this halachah is like Ra'avad. It would seem a great wonder on Rambam from this Tosefta — in my humble opinion one may say Rambam and his camp hold as Kesef Mishneh wrote: even though Torah obligated se'or though disqualified from a dog — when disqualified from its leavening having leavened so much it is unfit even for a dog, for this reason it is not considered like dust [like chametz into which something very sharp was poured and cannot eat from sharpness — for this it does not nullify the food there]; but if se'or spoiled so much it is unfit for a dog from molding — even if it had not leavened so much also unfit for a dog from mold and spoilage — it may be said this is considered dust, as Rif wrote therefore if it molded until disqualified from a dog one is not obligated in its destruction because it is dust — thus what if chametz became dust or se'or became dust; especially in Kesef Mishneh chapter 1 of Chametz and Matzah he wrote even per Ra'avad if se'or was disqualified from a dog's eating one is exempt for eating and obligated only in destruction because it is fit to leaven other doughs. Peri Megadim remained in doubt on his words from this Tosefta; per our words where unfit for a dog from mold Torah did not obligate as above — only for destruction Ra'avad obligates. Even if not like Kesef Mishneh but Ra'avad makes no distinction between eating and destruction — per Rambam and supporters the above reasoning fits: where from mold se'or is unfit for a dog one is obligated neither in eating nor destruction.",
    "9:ב": "Until it is unfit for a dog — and even though in other prohibitions whatever is unfit for human eating is permitted, chametz is different for even if unfit for human eating it is fit to leaven other doughs [Magen Avraham in the name of Ran]."
  },
  "output/siman_442/chok-yaakov/part-001.txt": {
    "1:א": "Chametz mixture. The agreement of Acharonim is like the Poskim: even without a kezayit within k'dei achilat peras one transgresses bal yera'eh; only min b'she'eino mino is not nullified d'oraisa in majority per those who hold ta'am ke'ikar d'oraisa; but min b'mino is nullified d'oraisa in majority and permitted to eat — all the more permitted to retain d'oraisa; however d'rabbanan forbidden to retain lest one eat from it on Pesach and must destroy; if one transgressed and retained, permitted after Pesach since he did not transgress bal yera'eh and bal yimatzei (as explained below siman 447 and 466 in Acharonim). Likewise where there are sixty even min b'she'eino mino the law is so. Where there is no substance of chametz only taste of chametz alone — Beit Yosef also wrote one transgresses bal yera'eh and bal yimatzei. In my opinion one should doubt this law greatly for the Poskim' implication is not so; likewise Minchat Kohen Sefer Ta'aruvot 63b. It appears even per those who hold ta'am ke'ikar d'oraisa without substance — that is only for eating learned from mesharet; but to transgress bal yera'eh since there is no form of chametz at all — and although Beit Hillel derive destruction from eating at the beginning of Beitzah 7b, nevertheless not all matters are equated; see Rashba responsum 30 who wants to permit one who borrows thus even in eating through sixty; likewise clear from Rav Netronai cited in Tur this siman (as in responsa Maharit chelek 68 siman 1). Tur wrote: but if there is taste of chametz one is obligated — that refers to eating not retaining; see below it refers to all where chametz substance is mixed without a kezayit within k'dei achilat peras — not as Bach wrote; likewise Bach and Rosh Yosef; Beit Yosef's words are astonishing (what is astonishing in Tur and Beit Yosef in this siman see Acharonim who elaborated — I raised what emerges for halachah). Greater still Beit Yosef wrote also what was cooked in a ben yomo vessel one should be stringent not to retain — I do not know whence this stringency; on the contrary Agudah beginning Pesachim explicitly wrote because of absorbed vessels no destruction needed; especially per many Poskim in all chametz mixtures one does not transgress ben yomo; also per Rashi and supporters ta'am ke'ikar is not d'oraisa; some say before Pesach its permitted time is called nosen ta'am bar nosen ta'am for absorbed vessels — all the more per what is explained below siman 447 per Rashi and Tosafos Pesachim Kol Sha'ah that absorbed vessels are only a rabbinic prohibition of something nullified before Pesach — thus one may say also taste and not substance of bal yera'eh; nevertheless in absorbed vessels no stringency at all and permitted to retain. Remains taste without substance — it appears in any case forbidden to retain d'rabbanan and requires destruction if found within Pesach; if one transgressed and retained no prohibition; in any case permitted in benefit; eating prohibition here is only a general stringency; likewise all permitted d'oraisa to retain but d'rabbanan forbidden l'chatchila and burn within Pesach d'rabbanan; if transgressed and retained permitted as below siman 447; likewise Magen Avraham; Mahari Tait ruled the famous Gaon Chami Zekeni z\"l cited in my brother-in-law's book Or Zarua siman 447 s.k. 21: butter in which bread and flour soaked and he retained within Pesach — take benefit of bread or flour to the sea and permit the butter; it appears flour was semolina or water mixed with butter for otherwise fruit juice does not leaven; our beer one transgresses bal yera'eh and bal yimatzei as Rosh wrote because its essence is barley — taste and substance and also for taste as Rosh general rule 2 regarding chadash — see there.",
    "1:ב": "They transgress regarding it. Shulchan Aruch's language is Rambam's who ruled like R' Eliezer: on chametz mixtures a lav; on chametz nokshah without anything — therefore he did not also write they transgress on chametz nokshah like the plain mishnah of Elu Overin; likewise explained below siman 447 seif 11; nevertheless l'chatchila forbidden to retain — see what is written there.",
    "1:ג": "And Median beer. All through water mixture for otherwise fruit juice does not leaven — so Yerushalmi; likewise Rosh; although water with fruit juice is chametz nokshah — Tosafos already noted Pesachim 35b s.v. mei peirot and resolved they soak wheat or barley first in water that leavens — see there; Tosafos' point escaped Rosh Yosef.",
    "1:ד": "And all the like in these matters — red vinegar and Egyptian olives, all listed in mishnah beginning Elu Overin; also burnt wine from sour produce called malts as in responsa Mishna Berurah siman 58 — full chametz and worse than chametz mixture — see there; likewise Rambam in tikkunin in printed responsum beginning Rif Krakow edition Seder Nashim — see there; no distinction between from sour produce or from beer lees; not as one sage thought — I will cite his question to me by name.",
    "1:ה": "And it is unfit for eating — permitted to retain. We do not decree lest one eat since entirely unfit for eating; not so chametz nokshah forbidden to retain l'chatchila even if not human food — nevertheless fit for eating under duress — Acharonim in the name of the Poskim.",
    "1:ו": "The form of chametz was lost. In Kol Bo he wrote Rambam's view; Ra'avad disagrees — permitted even to eat within Pesach since already nullified before its time — end of his words; this is Ra'avad's intent in hasagot chapter 5 of Chametz laws except his language is garbled from a printing error; Maggid Mishneh and Lechem Mishneh did not reach his intent in this hasagah — certainly his intent as Kol Bo explicitly in his name; learn the concealed from the explicit; what the Rav wrote nearby seif 4 in the interwoven gloss and below refers to all these the Poskim taught here — end of his words.",
    "2:א": "And melogma — explained as a poultice of flour and figs and the like that a person chews and places on a wound; Rambam in mishnah commentary chapter 8 of Shevi'it mishnah 1 and chapter 24 of Kelim mishnah 3: melogma is a full logma — he chews wheat on his wound — see siman 466.",
    "2:ב": "That putrefied. Only when it putrefied before Pesach, or putrefied and afterward became chametz; likewise chametz that molded as in seif 9.",
    "3:א": "That they laundered them. Rokeach siman 266: clothes soaked in bran after laundering may be worn.",
    "3:ב": "And likewise papers. Maggid Mishneh: this is not what the Gemara mentions — kolan shel sofrim forbidden to retain before papers were glued when it is nokshah b'eyna; our Rabbi's words are after papers were glued — like a kneading-trough to which he put hides — end of his words. Tur in the name of Ravyah — different distinction: kolan shel sofrim — the paper itself is improved thus chametz is recognizable; but we glue parchment on the tablet and it is not recognizable — permitted — end of his words. In the Mechaber's words following Rambam both explanations apply to what Machatzit and Ravyah dispute (not as Bach in Beit Yosef's name that Rambam himself disputes Ravyah). Taz s.k. 2: it is astonishing Ravyah's distinction — if chametz prohibition here, what helps covering with paper? bal yera'eh applies even buried in a pit not visible; he is again astonished at Terumat HaDeshen who followed Ravyah and raised many laws unlike them — see his words at length; in my humble opinion no wonder at all — kolan shel sofrim is chametz nokshah; even if chametz recognizable one does not transgress ben yomo as above Poskim conclude — nokshah b'eyna without anything; nevertheless forbidden to retain lest one eat; only when chametz recognizable; not so parchment glued on a tablet — chametz not recognizable — no concern one will eat; no ben yomo prohibition — this is plainly Ravyah's and Taz's intent; Taz thought Ravyah holds bal yera'eh therefore wondered even buried in pit one transgresses — in truth not so.",
    "3:ג": "Permitted to retain them. Magen Avraham: nevertheless forbidden to spread those clothes laundered in wheat milk (called in foreign tongue scharaff) on the table or put Pesach flour in them; also women's scarves usually fixed with flour should be removed at eating lest some crumble into food — end of his words. Practice with clothes laundered in wheat milk: wear at eating; no concern for nothing crumbles as is not customary; Magen Avraham deals where they fix them with flour until somewhat thick that crumbs are customary as his language implies.",
    "3:ד": "And there are those stringent if visible outside. Not done thirty days prior; Baer Heitev there: stringent coat outside with clay; meticulous remove all dough and fix windows before Pesach.",
    "4:א": "Forbidden to eat and permitted in benefit.",
    "4:ב": "And so is established. Taz wrote: even per those who disagree, forbid eating here lest vinegar establishes it — nullification before Pesach does not help — end of his words; all this because he did not see Ra'avad's words I brought above s.k. 6; Taz's doubt does not remove Ra'avad's certainty and Rama.",
    "5:א": "Obligated to destroy. Contains karet and bal yera'eh (if a kezayit within k'dei achilat peras); if mixed in something else forbidden like other chametz mixtures. Maharil siman 97 [93].",
    "5:ב": "If he established cheese in vinegar. Likewise if they pour chametz, beer, or burnt wine into it to establish it — also davar hama'amid; in responsum Nachalat Sheva siman 35: if they did not sell before Pesach cheese into which burnt wine was poured — forbidden in benefit because done for taste and not nullified; likewise Maharam of Lublin in dough leavened in beer lees that passed Pesach; agreement of Acharonim siman 447 seif 11 — see there; likewise gloss of Maharal Katzenellenbogen here in the name of Rav Maharan Katz in honey water established with beer lees; likewise Rashba responsum 499 even if established honey water in lees of honey water already established in beer lees; see Taz who elaborated honey water practice and conclusion: some are lenient to drink on last Yom Tov of Pesach mei einayim of all year as Rama 447 — likewise anything cooked in chametz vessel lenient on last Yom Tov; it appears because of doubt if ben yomo when cooked — we hold stam vessels are not ben yomo; on last Yom Tov we say so even for chametz; but if doubt perhaps leavened in beer lees no leniency; all the more where they cook mei einayim all year in cauldrons making burnt wine — sharp strong praised in mei einayim — known that not ben yomo helps — no doubt — guard your soul, distance even on last Yom Tov; all the more buying mei einayim from gentile on last Yom Tov lest beer mixed — end of his words. Although davar hama'amid and l'ta'amai are rabbinic as Tosafos Kol HaZevachim and Or Zarua — nevertheless since davar hama'amid is like b'eyna forbidden even b'dieved if retained until after Pesach; possibly Tur and Magen Avraham's view that davar hama'amid is d'oraisa not nullified — Beit Hilul: asked about a vessel (maruge/tish) for kneading — se'or left stuck, forgot washing and sale, Pesach passed, kneaded again — I answered bread permitted as Orach Chayim 447 seif 18 — if stuck on walls less than sixty throw money to river; se'or l'ta'amai even in thousand not nullified though dough nullifies se'or per Terumat HaDeshen siman 100 — thus se'or nullified though l'ta'amai; Chullin 39 se'or different in 101 but in thousand nullifies — his words here very astonishing contradict themselves and proof from Terumat HaDeshen against Gemara and all Poskim that spice forbids in any amount as Rambam beginning chapter 16 and universal agreement; even rabbinic prohibitions as Beit Yosef siman 112 and Acharonim siman 99 Peri Chadash s.k. 7-8; Terumat HaDeshen proof refuted — only dough not nullified relative to se'or for challah; se'or certainly not nullified per Poskim; if se'or insufficient to leaven it nullifies as Yoreh De'ah 98:1 and 87 both causes permitted if prohibition alone cannot leaven — Acharonim as Taz and Shach and Peri Chadash; l'ta'amai forbidden while taste sensed Yoreh De'ah 98:8 Peri Chadash 124; nevertheless perhaps all l'ta'amai cases not nullified or davar hama'amid no worse than less than sixty chametz that passed Pesach — casting prohibition benefit to Dead Sea as 447 seif 11 gloss; some Acharonim forbid entirely as b'eyna Ramban siman 8 in Magen Avraham siman 5 — investigate; in great loss may permit benefit by casting establishing benefit to Dead Sea; Acharonim in Radbaz: cheese in stomach absorbed from chametz vessel — forbidden to eat, permitted to retain.",
    "7:_": "And if not he must destroy. Meaning through combination there is a kezayit; if not he is not obligated in destruction; so Mechaber's seif 8; plainly in psakim of Mahari siman 164 less than kezayit no destruction needed; in truth dispute among Poskim — many hold even less than kezayit requires destruction; see Beit Yosef and Bach (and above siman 438 s.k. 10) who elaborated; Taz wrote: per reason we fear lest one eat — even less than kezayit obligated even without ben yomo from Chachamim's stringency as Tosefta beginning Pesachim in Rashba's name; no need to elaborate — Rosh wrote no need to explain these laws for Israel are holy and scrape all chametz found even stuck to house walls — see seif 11 in the gloss.",
    "9:_": "Or they burned in fire. Rosh and Almudi who make morayis and place roasted bread in it — some say permitted after Pesach: chametz through mixture that passed Pesach is permitted; also it was charred before its time and permitted in benefit after its time; some great ones say we do not rule thus — R' Shimon permits only like mixture unintentionally but not to mix and retain; also from charring perhaps not charred well — he did not have Pesach in mind at making; they did not say charred before its time permitted in eating after its time only in benefit — not customary to eat fully charred bread; but morayis is customary thus forbidden — end of Rosh. Magen Avraham wonders on Tur implying he ruled like those who say; in truth per those who say as Rosh wrote forbidden even in benefit — why did Tur rule forbidden in eating and left in wonder; to me more wonder: Tur's intent rules like first view forbidden to mix and retain for Tur explicitly ruled elsewhere no lav in chametz mixture only rabbinic; therefore permitted to mix l'chatchila before Pesach and retain (though Tur concluded investigate there is no taste of chametz in these put only for fermentation; but if taste of chametz d'oraisa ta'am ke'ikar and kezayit within k'dei achilat peras — if mixed automatically forbidden per Tur as below siman 447 end); also Tur 447 explicitly disagrees with those who forbid mixing l'chatchila to retain; therefore it appears one resolves the other: Tur rules first view permitted in benefit forbidden in eating as his summary in Rosh beginning Kol Sha'ah — see there; still wonder: perhaps first view itself forbids eating for chametz through mixture permitted even in eating after Pesach as Tur 447; one may say first view itself doubts if called chametz through mixture lest taste ke'ikar or kezayit within k'dei achilat peras. Shulchan Aruch omitted this law entirely — possibly because Rosh and supporters follow their method that ruled no bal yera'eh in chametz mixture; Mechaber here follows Rambam that morayis is chametz mixture category transgressing ben yomo; this morayis too — and all these laws see what is written this siman.",
    "10:_": "Permitted to write with it. Terumat HaDeshen siman 159: we are not concerned he puts quill in mouth; chametz disqualified from dog forbidden in eating in any case since placed without intent no concern — only when intended for eating forbidden for he designated it for eating — end of his words; it appears therefore plainly if ink or other chametz disqualified from dog fell into food even in majority it is nullified since it is mere dust and he did not designate it for eating — intent only to eat the food; this is entirely spoiled from the outset; see siman 447. Likewise printed ink made from chametz crumbs very spoiled; ink a gentile cooked on Pesach forbidden to write since not disqualified from dog before its time — so Terumat HaDeshen; see Yoreh De'ah siman 134.",
    "11:א": "For impossible to scrape. It appears only washing and scraping do not help but scraping (gerirah) helps; thereby resolve Taz's difficulty on Tur's contradictory words; Tur's end language implies scraping does not help; likewise below siman 451 regarding hag'alah — see Taz and Magen Avraham who distinguish kneading-troughs kneaded in with bread between boards versus small troughs of one piece without crack — plainly may scrape; Mahariv siman 123; Maharil Pesach laws: custom to make rolling pins and all tools used in dough — sell to gentile or coat with clay; Mahariv concluded: hide in room not needed on Pesach; Magen Avraham: not tell gentile so but give absolute gift and after Pesach retrieve (see siman 478).",
    "11:ב": "Laundering. Magen Avraham: in hot water, ash, and beating; see siman 451 law of sacks for Pesach flour as written there."
  },
  "output/siman_442/eliyah-rabbah/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "(1) [Levush] And there are those who say one is obligated, etc. Shulchan Aruch ruled stam like this view; Kesef Mishneh chapter 4 agreed even without kezayit within k'dei achilat peras since there is a kezayit of chametz one transgresses bal yera'eh. Magen Avraham: if min b'mino such as flour in flour nullified in majority d'oraisa permitted even to retain d'oraisa — end of his words; if transgressed and retained — if remembered within Pesach destroy d'rabbanan; if not see end siman 447 after Pesach. Olat Shabbat: fat from a dish with only taste not substance transgresses bal yera'eh — ta'am ke'ikar d'oraisa — end of his words; my brother-in-law in Chok Yaakov: ta'am ke'ikar only for eating not retaining; d'rabbanan forbidden to retain; if transgressed permitted in benefit after Pesach.",
    "2:_": "(2) But a thing, etc., that also prohibition, etc. Nokshah forbidden to retain though rabbinic as end siman 447 — must say mixtures were lenient more than nokshah b'eyna — not like Olat Shabbat.",
    "3:_": "(3) And likewise kilyor, etc., permitted to retain, etc. But in eating forbidden to all — Rambam deals where there are not sixty. This resolves my brother's wonder on Maggid regarding Ra'avad — if Ra'avad here he should have written hasagah end chapter 4 when nullified in sixty — what Levush seif 4 wrote — see there — end of his words.",
    "4:_": "(4) And melogma, etc. — poultice of flour and figs chewed on a wound; or chews wheat; deals with putrefaction unfit even for dogs like mold; but if fit for dogs though unfit for man — obligated to destroy.",
    "5:_": "(5) [Levush] Before its time, etc. After its time must burn to dust; only if leavened before putrefaction or molded; but putrefied before leavened — even after its time permitted to retain for prohibition did not apply.",
    "6:_": "(6) Clothes, etc. Nevertheless forbidden to spread on table; all the more forbidden to put flour in them on Pesach; women's scarves fixed with flour remove at eating lest crumble into food (Magen Avraham).",
    "7:_": "(7) That there is no form, etc. Terumat HaDeshen: not outside; though bal yera'eh even in hiding — chametz nokshah different (Chok Yaakov); Levush concluded: unfit for dogs, etc. — see seif 6.",
    "8:_": "(8) [Levush] And there are those who permit in mixture, etc. At first glance wonder on theriac — plainly chametz mixture; must say only theriac which is a mixed thing; but where chametz form stands as mentioned — forbidden to all; not precise why say nullified in sixty — not applicable; it appears to exclude intentional mixture to retain — forbidden as 447 seif 2 explained there.",
    "9:_": "(9) That there are, etc., nullified, etc. My brother challenged in Chok Yaakov 447: Tur ruled does not revive — still ruled Rambam's law in theriac; must say agrees theriac revives since chametz substance inside not well mixed as Rosh Yosef — end of his words. I found in Perishah explicitly Tur does not hold like Rambam in theriac — brought only to prove distinction eating vs revival; likewise explicit as Rama and Levush in Maggid end chapter 4 — those who permit below permit here; Kesef Mishneh 447; Taz wrote in theriac not nullified because ma'amid — Poskim above escaped him — see 447 s.k. 10.",
    "10:_": "(10) Beer, etc. If mixed in something else judged like other chametz mixture.",
    "11:_": "(11) If he established, etc. Davar hama'amid not nullified even in thousand; likewise pouring chametz, beer, or burnt wine to make sharp — even after cheese made and established in stomach — l'ta'amai not nullified; even retained after Pesach forbidden Nachalat Sheva 35. Chok Yaakov: davar hama'amid and l'ta'amai rabbinic — nevertheless like b'eyna forbidden if retained after Pesach; possibly no worse than less than sixty chametz passed Pesach — cast benefit to Dead Sea as 447:11; Magen Avraham in Ramban — all forbidden; in great loss lenient in benefit to sea — end of his words. Wonder: Ramban only forbidden to retain, if transgressed and retained not found forbidden since rabbinic; Maharam Lublin forbids dough leavened in beer lees passed Pesach — different for lees b'eyna; found in Amar Kol of Ravyah: honey leavened in beer lees forbidden to retain; if transgressed forbidden after Pesach — eating d'oraisa like paste — end of his words. Plainly if also permitted honey both causes permitted; if alone leavened see Yoreh De'ah end 87. L'ta'amai only when much given until taste as Yoreh De'ah end 98. Davar hama'amid needs power to leaven. Radbaz: cheese in stomach from chametz vessel — forbidden to eat, permitted to retain — only if stomach constantly in chametz vessel as Yoreh De'ah end 87 (Magen Avraham); needs study — nosen ta'am lifgam after constant contact; possibly sharp — not clear there; see 447:5 fat in chametz vessel. Magen Avraham in Ramban responsum 8: honey cooked now fourth or fifth from honey leavened in beer lees — forbidden; likewise Ra'avan 72b. I wondered in Eliyah Zuta end 447 why he permitted Knesset HaGedolah to make third milk from chametz — see there at length; for halachah may be lenient if transgressed and retained."
  },
  "output/siman_442/kaf-hachayyim/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "(1) [Seif 1] Chametz mixture they transgress, etc. — one does not receive lashes for bal yera'eh and bal yimatzei unless he acquired chametz on Pesach or leavened so an action occurs; if he had chametz before Pesach and Pesach came and he did not destroy but left in his domain — though he transgressed two lavin he does not receive lashes d'oraisa for no action was done — he receives makat mardut. Rambam chapter 1 law 3 and method of correction for one who transgressed — see above siman 434.",
    "2:_": "(2) There. Chametz mixture they transgress, etc. — Shulchan Aruch follows Rambam chapter 4 who ruled like R' Eliezer: lav on chametz mixture, nothing on chametz nokshah — therefore did not write they transgress on nokshah like mishnah Elu Overin; likewise below 447:12 — see there; nevertheless l'chatchila forbidden to retain on Pesach. Chayei Adam letter 2; if retained not forbidden after Pesach as below 447:12 — see there.",
    "3:_": "(3) And Maggid Mishneh wrote there Rambam ruled chapter 1 no lav in eating chametz mixture unless he ate a kezayit of chametz within k'dei achilat peras (three eggs as Rambam chapter 1; Shulchan Aruch — some say four eggs as above 468:3 and 472:4 — see there); certainly per his words here too one transgresses only in mixture with kezayit within k'dei achilat peras — this is primary. Some Geonim hold even with less one transgresses; I saw one who explains Rambam so — though no lav in eating obligated in destruction for kezayit of chametz is there though mixed in many olives — see there; likewise our Rabbi in Kesef Mishneh: for this no distinction whether kezayit within k'dei achilat peras or not — see there. Olat Shabbat letter 1: as Ran and primary. Magen Avraham s.k. 1; Rabbi Akiva Eiger letter 1; Chayei Adam letter 1: agreement of Acharonim like Poskim even without kezayit within k'dei achilat peras transgresses bal yera'eh; Makor Chayim letter 1; Gra 7 letter 1; Chayei Adam klal 121 letter 1; only min b'she'eino mino not nullified d'oraisa in majority per ta'am ke'ikar d'oraisa; min b'mino (e.g. flour of sour produce in flour not leavened) nullified d'oraisa in majority permitted to eat — all the more to retain d'oraisa; d'rabbanan forbidden to retain lest eat on Pesach — destroy; if transgressed permitted after Pesach (even in eating — Gra 13) since no ben yomo (as below 447 and 466 Acharonim). Where sixty — even min b'she'eino mino likewise. Chayei Adam there; Chok Yosef letter 1; see below 447 letter 244. Wheat flour with barley flour etc. is min b'she'eino mino. Rabbi Akiva Eiger letter 1.",
    "4:_": "(4) And if no substance of chametz only taste (e.g. dish cooked in one vessel with chametz absorbed taste without substance — Gra) — Olat Shabbat: forbidden to retain on Pesach and transgresses ben yomo; Chok Yosef agrees; Chayei Adam: Poskim' implication not so; even ta'am ke'ikar d'oraisa only for eating from mesharet not bal yera'eh; concludes in any case forbidden to retain d'rabbanan — destroy if found within Pesach; if transgressed no prohibition; permitted in benefit; eating prohibition only general stringency; likewise all permitted d'oraisa to retain — d'rabbanan forbidden l'chatchila — burn within Pesach d'rabbanan; if transgressed permitted. Makor Chayim letter 1; Gra 7; Chayei Adam klal 121 letter 1.",
    "5:א": "(5) Olat Shabbat further: also cooked in ben yomo vessel (cooked today in pot that cooked chametz today) — stringent not to retain on Pesach — see there; Chayei Adam disagreed — no stringency — permitted to retain — see there; Makor Chayim; Gra 8; Chayei Adam klal 121 letter 1; Chatam Sofer responsum 108: in sharp forbidden to retain — other matters permitted — see there. Makor Chayim: only if not kavush within Pesach in chametz vessel — poured to Pesach vessel — meaning vessel fit for Pesach use not used hot for chametz only cold as below 451:22 — see there; see Har HaCarmel 14: preserves or fat standing in chametz pots permitted to retain — see there; brought Sha'arei Teshuvah letter 1.",
    "5:ב": "(5) And if cooked or pickled on Pesach even in vessel not ben yomo — must destroy. Chayei Adam there. This per those who forbid ta'am lifgam on Pesach as below 447:10 in gloss; per Shulchan Aruch there permitted even in eating as explained there in our words — see there letter 241 for practical halachah.",
    "6:_": "(6) Tur: whatever not forbidden in eating d'oraisa permitted to mix l'chatchila and retain. Semag likewise; Tosafos beginning Pesachim: forbidden — Rabbanan did not distinguish. Magen Avraham s.k. 1; Shulchan Aruch's view — see there; see below 447 letter 241.",
    "7:_": "(7) In min b'mino even moist in moist less than sixty permitted to retain. Chametz that establishes — even b'dieved forbidden after Pesach. Magen Avraham here and 447:11. Makor Chayim letter 1.",
    "8:_": "(8) There. They transgress, etc. — Israel with mills grinding all year semolina, etc. — must destroy all; if not transgresses bal yera'eh and bal yimatzei. Radbaz responsa printed in Furth 489 from author letter 2.",
    "9:_": "(9) Such as morayis and Babylonian paste, etc. — Babylonian paste: they crumble bread crumbs to leaven; Median beer: barley and water leaven. Tur. Morayis also has chametz mixture. Levush.",
    "10:_": "(10) There. Such as morayis and paste, etc. — all through water mixture for otherwise fruit juice does not leaven. Rif, Rosh, Bach, Beit Yosef. See below 462.",
    "11:_": "(11) There. And all the like, etc. — red vinegar, Egyptian olives, all in mishnah beginning Elu Overin. Chayei Adam letter 4."
  },
  "output/siman_442/peri-megadim/part-001.txt": {
    "1:א": "Ta'aruvot (chametz mixtures). Taz: what he wrote on Beit Yosef — per Beit Yosef's view his words fit one who said that in the Bavli the girsa is Rav Nachman and likewise chametz nokshah, and they emended it — see Maharsha z\"l in Tosafos s.v. velu and there: Rav Nachman — why did he not speak like R' Yosi? That we hold possibly etc. — it indicates the old girsa: per Rav Nachman chametz nokshah at any rate is not less severe than ta'aruvot and he brings it in any case; and possibly nokshah is more severe, therefore Rav Nachman is not decided to say it is R' Eliezer; but Rav Yehudah rejects explicitly and says if so etc. — per him ta'aruvot is certainly more severe than nokshah, and it was taught thus — this contradicts Rav Nachman's words who brings it to R' Eliezer in any case from the braita that lists four: paste, beer, vinegar, and olives, and does not list one item and rely on the others in any case — all the more nokshah should have been taught and not rely in any case — learn that for R' Eliezer nokshah has nothing to it; and Rashi s.v. Rav Nachman holds in truth nokshah is stricter and there is no baraita like him, for it is all the more so (see there in Tosafos s.v. velu and Maharsha z\"l and understand). And so too per what Rav Nachman wrote likewise — and therefore it is satisfactory that he does not bring from the above braita, for certainly likewise etc. — see Eshel Avraham part 1 Choshen Mishpat 6 and per what was written satisfactory. And one may also say that Rif z\"l exempts — ruled like the Rabbis from stam mishnah below 45b: se'or shall be burned and one who eats it — all the more ta'aruvot which is lighter than nokshah and like Rav Nachman and Or Zarua, and Bach z\"l answered that therefore in any case the mishnah is R' Meir — for if like Rav Nachman the mishnah does not follow R' Meir for nokshah is stricter — if so why did the braita not list R' Eliezer's nokshah to exclude from karet as Tosafos s.v. velu wrote — learn that nokshah and ta'aruvot are equivalent and the mishnah is like R' Meir and R' Eliezer can be satisfied; and as it appears this is also the Taz's intent, and he added a reason that presumably it is not satisfactory to say three disputes etc., and that which he did not say all these for R' Meir in the lav that he brings the dispute of the Rabbis.",
    "1:ב": "And what Taz wrote at length in Tur in Beit Yosef. Per Taz it is thus: the Rabbis argue on R' Eliezer in ta'aruvot when there is not a kezayit within k'dei achilat peras, and also there is no practical difference except keviyusa (foam — not considered a taste and not ta'am ke'ikar); per the Rabbis chametz is like other prohibitions and therefore permitted, and d'rabbanan forbidden in eating and retaining is permitted for it is a decree upon a decree per Tur; and likewise a thing that has no keviyusa at all and was nullified in sixty does not revive per Tur and is permitted on Pesach to eat — nevertheless forbidden to mix before Pesach and eat on Pesach for it is like nullifying a prohibition with intent to eat on Pesach; and even though it is still permitted and there is a doubt of four eggs per R' Yehuda 109 — that one increases up to sixty in dry for it was already nullified in majority, but to mix b'eyna not — see Taz siman 80 and what Peri Megadim wrote in Yoreh De'ah 98 in Shach note 22, or chametz is its name upon it and it deals with 447 in Taz note 5 and 426:2 and see there it will be explained; but to mix it to drink on Pesach is permitted per Tur for all say the Rabbis permit retaining — all the more this. But for us all agree what is forbidden d'rabbanan is forbidden to retain, and if he transgressed it is permitted after Pesach for he did not acquire it since he did not transgress bal yera'eh. And what Rosh wrote in a mixture from chadash — likewise d'rabbanan forbidden and requires study. And what he challenged on Mechaber Beit Yosef — see Derishah that Tur wrote explicitly ta'aruvot for R' Meir are only d'rabbanan and similar Bach wrote therefore permitted to retain per Tur — see there; in truth these are strong challenges, and to say what Taz wrote at the end refers to d'rabbanan prohibition applies to nokshah and likewise R' Yosi — and this is what he wrote at the beginning ta'aruvot etc. — all this is not equal to me. And to study Torah I need — Heaven forbid to suspect Mechaber Beit Yosef that he did not see Tur's words at the end and say that certainly what Tur wrote and for the Rabbis it is a mere prohibition, meaning a Torah prohibition, and that is from what he wrote afterward immediately and Rambam ruled like R' Eliezer — therefore as Beit Yosef wrote Tur explains per Rambam z\"l that R' Eliezer and the Rabbis argue on paste where there is a kezayit within k'dei achilat peras through lapping and through dipping there is not, and one eats it through lapping — for R' Eliezer we derive from kol machmetz for lashes and the Rabbis exempt from lashes for there is not etc., but there is a Torah prohibition — see Eshel Avraham part 5 foundations of Torah and on this Tur wrote a mere prohibition bal yera'eh, like half a measure that has neither lav nor positive — otherwise it should say forbidden d'rabbanan, and this is required for he wrote and Rambam ruled like R' Eliezer — therefore per Rambam permitted to retain them, and on this Beit Yosef wondered: since there is a Torah prohibition why permitted to retain. And what Tur wrote at the end for the Rabbis d'rabbanan prohibition is per himself likewise he establishes their dispute where there is not a kezayit within k'dei achilat peras at all. And in several places part 5 foundations of Torah the Gaon wrote that per the Rabbis one may say that per the Rabbis it is lighter than per R' Eliezer, and Pesachim 24b concludes all the more so per R' Eliezer — meaning what he placed fat etc. on his wound is all the more so, not like per the Rabbis actual eating is stricter — nevertheless part 8 letter 14 from Magen Avraham and understand.",
    "1:ג": "Or one may say: even if we hold per R' Eliezer there is no Torah prohibition in paste where there is a kezayit within k'dei achilat peras through lapping — nevertheless its taste and substance are called and considered and not nullified per R' Yosi — see 87:1. And now certainly per Rambam z\"l who establishes their dispute where there is a kezayit within k'dei achilat peras through lapping — per the Rabbis there is a Torah prohibition, not like Tur below per Rif who establishes where there is not a kezayit within k'dei achilat peras at all — if so there is Beit Yosef's challenge how Tur wrote for the Rabbis permitted to retain per Rambam z\"l — this cannot be justified. And see what I wrote in the introduction at the beginning of the book and in Levush siman 88 for Rambam letter 8 forbidden bal yera'eh — nevertheless all that there is a kezayit within k'dei achilat peras, not as written in the book of ta'aruvot; and that which says leave paste if from a harsh regime etc. — does not refer thus in the conclusion. And what Taz wrote: beer where there is no substance at all — our beer its taste and substance are called — see Eshel Avraham letter 8 only per Rashi and some Poskim call this taste and not substance — see 35 Taz; and we hold liable for karet on our beer on Pesach — see what I wrote in the introduction. And we have thoughts in Temim De'im on Ra'avad z\"l in his hasagot on Baal HaMaor siman 245 regarding Rif who ruled like the Rabbis in ta'aruvot for they are many and mishnah at the beginning of the chapter is stam like an individual, and nokshah he rules like stam mishnah at the beginning of the chapter — the content of his intent: whatever many argue in a baraita even though stam in mishnah is like an individual, halachah is not so as we say in general a lone mishnah; not so if in baraita R' Meir and R' Yosi and stam in mishnah like R' Meir — halachah is so, and I did not understand one thing: for it is also that R' Yosi, and if we say as Beit Yosef wrote that in truth halachah is like Rav Nachman for nokshah is stricter — still I did not find that Rif z\"l rules explicitly like R' Meir, and behold Tur writes the opposite and mishnah 45b and one who eats it is exempt and stam at the end is preferable — see Magen Avraham note 2 what he wrote there. And what he wrote to distinguish between retaining and eating — see part 4 letter 8. And Magen Avraham in the introduction here:",
    "2:א": "And likewise Taz: behold for us nokshah has no bal yera'eh per 447:11 and 453 and Magen Avraham note 1 and Chayei Adam — nevertheless Ra'avad implies he holds nokshah has bal yera'eh as mishnah at the beginning of the chapter — if so what is the challenge for us that they stick dough, for they hold nokshah is d'rabbanan in eating — such as where it is not appropriate to wonder; not so in mishnah where nokshah has bal yera'eh even though not fixed thereby bal yera'eh even when not visible forbidden (see what I wrote in the introduction at the beginning of the book and in Mishbetzot Zahav part 4 Choshen Mishpat — implies also not visible, and in any case not found) — on this Taz challenged well. And possibly even for us there is a Torah prohibition in nokshah in eating and retaining, and no lav and R' Shimon does not penalize — Magen Avraham note 1; and Mishna Berurah brings some are stringent — view of Ra'avya — printed here Taz z\"a y\"sh — see there.",
    "2:ב": "And behold we already elaborated in the introduction at the beginning of the book regarding ta'aruvot and kezayit within k'dei achilat peras — and if not that I fear authors I would say there is a new law: rice with chametz — see part 3 siman 447 illness and Rambam part 6 Choshen Mishpat and the author below 453 and Levush there and Beit Din who wrote specifically rice from the law of gerirah (scraping) — if so chametz flour from wheat mixed with rice flour and he made dough from them — even though there is only a little wheat, all became chametz and if one ate a kezayit from it he transgressed bal yera'eh and after Pesach forbidden in benefit and the lenient views do not help etc. — see 447 and Kaf HaChayyim note 1; nevertheless if rice flour fell into flour and he baked matzah in such a case one may say it does not leaven and there is no gerirah — see Magen Avraham 453 note 4 as Taz wrote note 9 and Shach 17 and see there in 462 I will explain further. Again I saw Taz's words are explained per their plain sense — Ra'avya possibly holds like Riva and these are eliminated; but from what Beit Yosef and Maggid Mishneh wrote — no need for Ra'avya's distinction, for it implies Ra'avya's distinction has a place in Rambam — wondrous, for Rambam ruled 4:11 that nokshah has a lav — plainly there is no Ra'avya distinction in Rambam z\"l, and the first view appears more correct.",
    "3:_": "And below Taz and Eshel Avraham notes 6 and 14. And safek davar hama'amid one may say it is a safek d'rabbanan for ma'amid is d'rabbanan; and one may say it is safek lacking knowledge; and it appears for retaining it is safek d'rabbanan and lenient, and for eating it is a double safek to forbid — perhaps it is ma'amid and perhaps halachah is like those who hold it revives — see note 4 and see there in 447 it will be explained further whether to join to be stringent safek perhaps it revives.",
    "4:א": "And likewise Taz: it appears that ma'amid while its power has not ceased within Pesach, or even though now its action finished — nevertheless we say it revives; but one who examines Shach Yoreh De'ah 14:87:35 — it appears in Bach that even d'rabbanan when recognizable there is no nullification in what is recognizable — what of it; whatever does not give taste the Torah did not forbid — not so hide of nevelah stomach; not so here on Pesach its action is recognizable — there is no nullification in what is recognizable, and even before Pesach it was not nullified but only permitted, and now forbidden from ma'amid; and practical difference: even though there are sides to permit — we rely on questions that on Pesach in sixty ma'amid does not apply — see Magen Avraham; and see Baer Heitev note 11 — requires study if they took from lees before Yom Tov — see there; nevertheless if they took after chatzot it is already ma'amid the prohibition by itself — also per Shach requires study; and in Biur HaGra I do not say neither prohibition nor permission, and for retaining one may side with Biur HaGra in such a case — still requires study also in this.",
    "4:ב": "And regarding honey water called mei einayim — if cooked in a ben yomo vessel from beer one may say it is nosen ta'am bar nosen ta'am of the permitted — see 447:65 permitted to retain and forbidden in eating; and if they cooked in a cauldron used all year for mei einayim one may say there is no nosen ta'am bar nosen ta'am in sharp things for the absorbed is sharp — see Pri Megadim and 426 there; and see Magen Avraham 447 likewise; and see there it will be explained. And in beer lees it is davar hama'amid; and what Taz wrote: safek Torah to be stringent — Magen Avraham note 9 that for halachah ma'amid is d'rabbanan and Eshel Avraham letter 5 here. Nevertheless if their custom is thus — there is no safek d'rabbanan for leniency. And see Magen Avraham note 9: in honey that was third or fourth — one may say beer lees mixed with honey lees is zuz gavra (this-and-that cause) and possibly permitted and nullified even though they did not take from lees before Pesach — all the more if they took before the night. And see Magen Avraham and Kaf HaChayyim.",
    "5:_": "And if Taz: what appears from his words that chametz nokshah has no bal yera'eh — there is a dispute — Eshel Avraham note 7; and in the introduction we wrote that for eating one may say it is fit to combine the explanation that perhaps he will eat a kezayit within k'dei achilat peras and think it is more than k'dei achilat peras — and in my view there is no such thing; and see Magen Avraham 447:2 and 4 and see there it will be explained. And what he wrote regarding lashes — it appears it is a lav she'ein bo ma'aseh — Rambam part 1 — requires study.",
    "6:_": "Since Taz — Magen Avraham note 12; and in several places part 2 Choshen Mishpat 17 they were stuck in cracks — it is asuy lechazek — see there; behold even half a kezayit alone one is obligated to destroy; and there is a doubt of 461:1 that in any case stuck in a kneading-trough even though not asuy lechazek — nevertheless it is stuck and nullified along with it when altogether there is not a kezayit — not so when half a kezayit lies without sticking on the ground. And see Magen Avraham note 12 and what he wrote there \"and plainly\" etc. And what Taz wrote: perhaps they will roll to each other — I did not understand, for the question is also with stuck ones — sometimes they gather through scrubbing, and therefore in one place one must destroy.",
    "7:_": "Rather Taz — Pesachim 45b, see Tur and Magen Avraham note 13 — and they were not nullified: it is a Torah doubt per Rambam z\"l with stuck ones that are asuy lechazek, and he is not concerned they will gather through scrubbing his house upon them; and per Rosh even without sticking and somewhat soiled — Magen Avraham note 12.",
    "8:_": "Permitted Taz: when a dog cannot eat — forbidden in eating d'rabbanan. And what he brought from Yoma there: when fit for a person he is exempt, and here disqualified from a person he is liable bal yera'eh — and se'or proves, for it is fit to leaven many doughs — Magen Avraham note 14; and disqualified from a dog — permitted in eating bal yera'eh, for it is no longer fit to leaven, or it is dust; and in benefit also permitted as stam yeinam; and Rava Pesachim 21b — see Rashi there that its taste and appearance were nullified — it appears not like Tosafos there s.v. charco and bread that molded and a dog can eat it — d'rabbanan obligated to destroy, for its appearance is chametz and one will confuse etc.; and chametz does not leaven — and there is a doubt for se'or — Magen Avraham note 14; and Mechaber who wrote to retain it — likewise in benefit permitted. And know that it is framed that retaining is permitted and benefit forbidden in nokshah disqualified from a person entirely and fit for a dog — lest one come to eat — therefore forbidden in benefit; and see part 4 letter 5; but in mixtures retaining is permitted — Magen Avraham note 1; and see there in 447:11 it will be explained in this.",
    "9:_": "Impossible to scrape Taz — one may say here it deals with boards and there with one board — Magen Avraham note 16. And what he wrote for halachah we do not hold thus — see 461:17 in Taz who wrote in Beit Yosef that because of custom they are stringent, for impossible to scrape well; and so appears Ra'avya's view there who wrote and it was already explained in Mishbetzot Zahav 11:1 — implies impossible to scrape; but Magen Avraham there note 33: in a house of se'or and in note 40 per his method — see there; and even in a crack if they regularly knead all year it is like a house of se'or; and per Magen Avraham one should not knead in it only those whose use is not regular — they are accustomed to scrape well for hag'alah and knead on Pesach; and if they bake in it in a scraped oven — bread needs libun as 461 in Magen Avraham note 28; and b'dieved if they kneaded on Pesach or before Pesach matzah in a chametz vessel that was not kashered — see 91 in Shach 3 — a sage showed me what Shach wrote and further that Rosh and Tur forbade kneading matzah only that it hastens to leaven — meaning other dough cold is permitted — implies b'dieved kneading matzah is permitted, for otherwise to forbid b'dieved; but it appears if he kneaded matzah dough in a vessel that was not kashered — even though we hold we require pressure and sharpness in chametz it hastens to leaven and b'dieved forbidden; and now requires study whether to forbid b'dieved — and see there 461:17 and other places I will explain further. And see Pri Megadim here note 19: if he transgressed after hag'alah and kneaded — there is room to permit — it implies before hag'alah not — Levush 461 left a little in the way possible; and see there 461 in Magen Avraham note 1 and Taz note 26 it will be explained further."
  },
  "output/siman_442/chatam-sofer/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "In Magen Avraham s.k. 1 s.v. ve'it appears if it became mixed, etc., and Beit Yosef's words require study. Note: in what he wrote for Tur according to Rabbanan he forbids in any case d'oraisa; Tur himself wrote for Rif there is only a rabbinic prohibition; also Tur wrote the reason for bedikah is because one transgresses bal yera'eh, and in siman 431 Tur himself wrote the reason lest one come to eat from it. Likewise Eliyah Zuta challenged.",
    "2:_": "In that s.k. and in Yoreh De'ah siman 134 he wrote in the name of Rashba regarding liquids. Note: see this responsum in Biur Shach Avodah Zarah 138a; Ran cites Ra'avad on the sugya of barrels in barrels in the Gemara 74a and see there 73a on the mishnah wine in water — in Yerushalmi where they sell water by measure, likewise wine; it appears to me it is a textual error and should read they mix water by measure and \"mix\" means dilute as in the Bavli 58b — see there; he holds whatever they dilute intentionally water is not nullified.",
    "3:_": "There in Magen Avraham s.k. 9 it says in siman 8. Note: should read Ra'avan siman 8 72a; what he wrote there stringently regarding benei cheila deals where they removed the se'or.",
    "4:_": "There in that s.k. and he permitted R' Yaakov. Note: son of Rabbenu Yitzchak — see there; this is not Rabbenu Tam; see responsa Chatam Sofer Yoreh De'ah siman 79.",
    "5:_": "There in that s.k. and only when the stomach. Note: see below siman 447 s.k. 16.",
    "6:_": "In the Mechaber seif 11 impossible to scrape. Note: if they kneaded matzah therein without scraping, hot crumbs of dough become se'or and there is concern the dough became chametz; even if they scrape, since they did not hag'alah the vessel's power leavens the dough — thus Peri Megadim in Eshel Avraham siman 451 s.k. 40; likewise Chayei Adam klal 125 letter 3."
  },
  "output/siman_442/chokhmat-shlomo/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "Seif 1: A chametz ta'aruvot (mixture) — they transgress regarding it, etc. Note: behold by law permitted combines to forbidden, such as when a minority of permitted mixed in a majority of forbidden if one is lashed for a kezayit of permitted and forbidden together; and see in my Chiddushei to Tractate Niddah daf 27b regarding whether two birds on one perch will rot on one perch and its measure increases — and there I proved that the minority permitted also combines with the majority for house tum'ah, and likewise for prohibition there is in this — let the challenger and answerer there be divided — examine. And behold examine Beit Yosef's language in this siman who wrote in the name of Machatzit HaShekel on our Rabbi's words in this language — a contradiction appears in the book's language from itself to itself, etc., with the end; and before I answer — behold this language is not understood, and also the body of his answer has no understanding. And it appears there is a textual error here, and initially it should read: and before I answer I will precede that the reason the Mechaber divided, etc., and his intent in this preface resolves the first difficulty: although he brought first that retaining is one level lighter than eating, he afterward brings Rambam's language that they transgress bal yera'eh on ta'aruvot — because initially Tur follows the commentators that \"they transgress\" means from the table; and for this explanation one may say retaining is lighter than eating on one side; but Rambam who explains \"they transgress\" as bal yera'eh — therefore he holds the law of destruction and eating are truly equal, and the first wonder is removed; and afterward it should read: and what can be explained in the permitted — the second contradiction, meaning he now comes to resolve the second difficulty, meaning what Rambam ruled like the Rabbis is for eating, but for bal yera'eh he ruled like the Rabbis and per him destruction is stricter than eating — per Maggid Mishneh's explanation; therefore for transgressing bal yera'eh even though there is not a kezayit within k'dei achilat peras, but for eating one is not lashed when there is not k'dei achilat peras — and with this he concludes, and the two contradictions I mentioned are resolved — the first contradiction resolved in the first preface and the second in this preface. So appears Machatzit HaShekel's intent; without this his words have no understanding — examine; and see Magen Avraham note 1 what he wrote on ta'aruvot chametz, etc., and Kesef Mishneh agreed to one view. And behold Beit Yosef explained further: for bal yera'eh Rambam ruled like R' Eliezer since stam mishnah is like him — meaning since in any case the chametz is in his house; but for eating Rambam ruled like the Rabbis. And I was asked by a man: if so what pushed the Gemara to say the mishnah is like R' Eliezer — behold one may say it is even like the Rabbis and they argue only for eating but for destruction they agree. And I answered him: there is room to say the beginning of the mishnah may be explained for bal yera'eh as Rashi explained — as a mishnah in any case; the conclusion that ends \"behold these are under a warning and have no karet\" must deal with eating, as Rashi himself explained; for if for bal yera'eh — what is it that he concluded \"and have no karet,\" behold even for chametz b'eyna there is no karet for bal yera'eh — why does it teach us this regarding ta'aruvot; also from the Torah it would be reasonable to say there is karet for bal yera'eh — and necessarily the conclusion deals with eating. Therefore from the conclusion it is required to say the mishnah is like R' Eliezer; and specifically the beginning is considered stam because since he stated stam first it is stam; but the conclusion \"behold these under a warning\" is not stam — one may say this is the conclusion of R' Eliezer's words who said first in the mishnah R' Eliezer says also women's ornaments — and all after this is R' Eliezer's words; therefore it is not considered stam; therefore Rambam ruled here like the Rabbis — and so it is, and plainly in my humble opinion — examine. And what Magen Avraham wrote citing Rashba: whatever one's intent is to do so is not nullified — see what I wrote on this in my composition Orach Chayim year 401 in my Chiddushei to Pesachim chapter 2 on the sugya that one does not cook the Pesach offering in water — see there what I wrote to wonder on him greatly — examine there; and see further what I wrote on these words of Magen Avraham in Yoreh De'ah siman 134 who wrote in the name of Rashba, etc., and we wrote to resolve his challenge in my Orach Chayim year 407 in my responsum to the community of Stanislav — find its place in hasagot to Sefer HaChayim, pamphlet Minchat Ta'amim — see there what I wrote — examine. And behold see Magen Avraham note 1 what he wrote: Maggid Mishneh chapter 4 wrote specifically when there is a kezayit of chametz within k'dei achilat peras — and this is primary. And one view says: even though one does not transgress in eating, he is obligated in destruction, and even though it is mixed in many olives he transgresses — behold there is a kezayit of chametz; and Kesef Mishneh agreed to this view — end quote Magen Avraham. And behold in my view it appears to decide like Kesef Mishneh from Yerushalmi chapter 1 of Beitzah halachah 2, and this is its language on the mishnah: Beit Shammai say se'or in a kezayit, etc. — and this is it: R' Avuh in the name of R' Yochanan: whether for destruction or for eating — a kezayit; R' Mana stood with R' Chizkiyah; he said to him: did you hear this matter? He said to him: from R' Avuh. He said to him: we also say R' Avuh in the name of R' Yochanan: whether for destruction or for eating — a kezayit; if not so, thirty-seven karetot should have been stated in the Torah — end quote. And behold this Yerushalmi is like the sealed book, and Kuntres Pnei Aryeh made a textual error and it should read: whether for destruction or for eating — in a kotevet (not kezayit). And behold apart from corrupting books, its explanation is also wondrous: one wonder is that he himself wondered on korbanot measures — what is the difficulty that perhaps it is not like Beit Shammai; and I wonder more: even without his proof there, that the mishnah was not like all, even if we say it was like all — there is difficulty: if it were a matter that could have been taught according to all, there would be difficulty why he inserted it in dispute — let him teach what is according to all; but here, if they are mistaken, there would be only thirty-seven karetot — it would be only like Beit Shammai, not like Beit Hillel; therefore in the end it would be according to the Tannaim — why abandon Beit Hillel's words and teach like Beit Shammai. And further wonder: behold there in Keritot he did not list measures, only species for which one is liable for karet and chatat — behold he counts \"one who eats chametz\" — what difference if one eats a kezayit of chametz or two olives; if so he could have taught that one ate several olives of chametz — necessarily because he only lists the species; and if so what difference chametz or se'or — what does it matter that this is kezayit and that kotevet. And it is strained to say since they argue about oil and law per Beit Shammai therefore he had to count them — this is strained to challenge thus — what is the difficulty. Therefore the girsa appears in its plain sense, and the answer is per what I wrote in my Chiddushei to Laws of Pesach siman 431, in my Orach Chayim year 409 daf 40 in explanation of Yerushalmi's language in chapter 5 of Pesachim — and the upshot from there: what Beit Hillel hold both in a kezayit and the Gemara explains the reason we derive destruction from eating — the intent is not through \"from where do we know\" for certainly there is no proof, for eating is stricter; rather the intent is through a fortiori: Beit Hillel hold therefore all measures are kezayit from the halachah leMoshe miSinai that eating language is kezayit, and automatically where eating is not written it is fortiori; if so for destruction, since eating is not written, it should be fortiori — only we derive leniency from eating, and leniency is properly derived from eating through a fortiori; and since we derive destruction from eating through fortiori and we require a measure also for destruction — automatically it is proper to say \"it is sufficient for what comes from the logical inference to be like its source\" — therefore also for destruction it is like kezayit — see there where I elaborated. And behold the upshot in this: it is in the dispute of Kesef Mishneh and Maggid Mishneh who derive destruction from eating \"from where do we know\" — if so it is proper to say: learn from it and establish it in its place: granted for eating, if not within k'dei achilat peras one does not eat together; but for destruction, since in the end the chametz is in his house — if through fortiori — likewise \"it is sufficient for what comes from the logical inference to be like its source\"; and since for eating one is liable only if mixed within k'dei achilat peras — likewise for destruction. And this is Yerushalmi's intent: behold if we say we derive destruction from eating \"from where do we know\" — let us also learn for karet to be liable for bal yera'eh. And one cannot say the truth is so, for in mishnah at the beginning of Elu Overin it teaches \"behold these are under warning and have no karet\" — from this alone it is not proof one may say it deals where there is not a kezayit within k'dei achilat peras, for then one cannot learn from eating, for in eating too one is exempt in such a case. And it could be said: learn from it and establish — even though not within k'dei achilat peras one transgresses; but if we say establish in its place — behold for it karet is not stated, only a lav; but where it is within k'dei achilat peras in a matter where for eating one is liable for karet — one may say also for destruction one is liable. Therefore Yerushalmi says this cannot be said, for otherwise it should teach thirty-seven karetot also for destruction where it is within k'dei achilat peras — necessarily for destruction in every case one is exempt from karet — necessarily we do not derive destruction from eating \"from where do we know\" but through fortiori, and automatically \"it is sufficient for what comes from the logical inference to be like its source,\" and one does not transgress bal yera'eh except within k'dei achilat peras — and this is what Yerushalmi says: whether for destruction or for eating — kezayit, meaning we require a kezayit together within k'dei achilat peras; and proof that necessarily Beit Hillel derive destruction from eating is necessarily through fortiori — for he said \"from where do we know\" — if so to be liable for karet also for destruction; and one cannot say the truth is so that one is liable if within k'dei achilat peras, and the mishnah that teaches \"behold these under warning\" deals with more than k'dei achilat peras — if so it should teach thirty-seven karetot — and it fits well in my humble opinion. And behold further proof for our words from what Semag wrote in Choshen Mishpat at the beginning of siman 72: although the majority is among the last, the first is plainer; and if we emend like Kuntres Pnei Aryeh's girsa \"whether for destruction or for eating — in a kotevet\" — then the first would be plainer; and it is strained to emend also this to read conversely \"whether for eating or for destruction — in a kotevet\" — this is so strained to emend; necessarily as I wrote — examine well.",
    "2:_": "There (seif 5): And likewise if he established cheese in vinegar of barley beer, etc. Note: see in Magen Avraham what he wrote in the name of Ramban's responsum, etc. It is clear from his words that even on the seventh or fifth there is still prohibition from the first beer; and see in my Chiddushei Yoreh De'ah siman 115 at the end of the siman what I wondered in Beit Yosef's and Taz's words there and what is difficult from there to here; and see explanation of his words in my humble opinion there — they are wondrous; also I proved there that zuz gavra (this-and-that cause) applies only if it was actually done now through two causes — but if in truth it was done only through the prohibition, yet if without the prohibition it would have become so by itself over many days — nevertheless it is not considered zuz gavra, for we follow the present and now it comes only through the prohibition and is considered only a cause of prohibition — examine there.",
    "3:_": "There (seif 9): Chametz that molded before its time of prohibition and was disqualified from a dog's eating, etc. Note: see in Magen Avraham note 9 what he wrote that Tur's view is that davar hama'amid is not nullified from the Torah; and see what I challenged on this in my composition Mei Niddah in hasagot still in manuscript page 173 — see there what proof I brought not like Tur — examine. And behold Maggid Mishneh in Laws of Chametz was in doubt how we hold eating in a kezayit — whether the intent is that Torah language is in a kezayit, or Torah language is in a fortiori measure and only the halachah leMoshe miSinai is that one is lashed only for a kezayit. I already spoke much of this in my humble opinion in several places. And now it arose in my mind to resolve this doubt from the beginning of Beitzah (daf 7): Beit Shammai say se'or in a kezayit and chametz in a kotevet (large measure) — why not write se'or? And it is difficult: whence do we learn leniently — perhaps stringently, that chametz is in a kezayit and se'or in a fortiori measure? And it is satisfactory if the halachah leMoshe miSinai is that eating language in Torah is in a kezayit and here eating language is said regarding se'or — it cannot be said it is fortiori, for it is written eating and necessarily it is lenient that chametz is in a kotevet; but if eating language is fortiori and only the halachah leMoshe miSinai is that one is lashed only for a kezayit — one may say stringently: the verse reveals here that for se'or one is lashed even in fortiori measure, for the halachah leMoshe miSinai does not apply to se'or, for even now the halachah leMoshe miSinai does not apply to all of them, for the halachah leMoshe miSinai is in a kezayit and here in a kotevet — necessarily the halachah leMoshe miSinai does not apply to chametz, and if so one may say the reverse: the halachah leMoshe miSinai does not apply to se'or and one is lashed per Beit Shammai — necessarily eating language is in a kezayit, meaning the halachah leMoshe miSinai is only that eating language is not less than a kezayit and only to exclude less — but the intent may be for more than a kezayit; therefore Beit Shammai hold chametz in a kotevet — and this is clear proof in my humble opinion. And afterward when it says they argue in destruction — one cannot challenge: here eating is not written and one may say se'or in fortiori measure — this is no question, for if so destruction would be stricter than eating, and this is not so, for eating is stricter, for for eating one is liable for karet and for destruction only a lav. Therefore it cannot be explained thus. But per my view in eating it would have been proper to say so and learn stringently — necessarily it is proven as I wrote, and this is correct in my humble opinion — examine. It also appears in my humble opinion to resolve this doubt of Maggid Mishneh from what Rambam wrote in Laws of Tum'at Met chapter 2 law 2: \"Even though all their measures are halachah leMoshe miSinai, the Sages said: the beginning of man's formation is in a kezayit; therefore the measure of tum'ah of his flesh is a kezayit\" — end quote. And behold these words have no explanation, since it is halachah leMoshe miSinai thus — why a statement of the Sages; therefore it is proven from this that Rambam holds that if eating language were fortiori, the halachah leMoshe miSinai would have no power to reduce so one is liable only for a kezayit — rather the halachah leMoshe miSinai is that Torah language itself is eating in a kezayit; if so it fits eating language — but for tum'ah where \"adam\" is written and no hint it is a kezayit — if adam implies fortiori measure, the halachah leMoshe miSinai would have no power to say it is like a kezayit; therefore they said initially adam at the beginning of his formation was a kezayit — therefore there is also halachah leMoshe miSinai for tum'ah as for eating: the halachah leMoshe miSinai is that adam language is a kezayit, for such was the beginning of his formation — a kezayit — and it is literally as in several places the halachah leMoshe miSinai reveals the intent of the written text in eating language; so too here the halachah leMoshe miSinai reveals the intent of adam language. And even if we say adam in the simple sense means a complete person — nevertheless it fits well that the intent is thus: it appears the halachah leMoshe miSinai is not similar, for regarding all measures the halachah leMoshe miSinai is to be lenient, for all halachah leMoshe miSinai is even as fortiori measure, and the halachah leMoshe miSinai comes to be lenient; and here it would be the reverse, for Scripture implies specifically every adam defiles in a tent but a kezayit does not defile — and the halachah leMoshe miSinai comes to be stringent; therefore he said the halachah leMoshe miSinai is equal everywhere — it comes to explain the intent of Scripture: there he explains Scripture's intent that eating language is a kezayit, and here he explains that adam language is a kezayit. In any case it is proven that the halachah leMoshe miSinai is to explain Scripture's intent — otherwise why do I need the Sages' statement for halachot leMoshe miSinai — and it fits well — examine. And behold I already brought Ra'avad's words above in this siman; and see Magen Avraham; and see Makor Chayyim of the Gaon of Lissa z\"l in this siman; and in my composition on Laws of Pesach what I settled Tur's words in this siman — see there. And behold I was asked by a scholar who challenged: if Tur holds the reason for the prohibition of retaining is lest one come to eat from it — benefit should also be forbidden; and if he holds from bal yera'eh — even eating should be permitted. And this is what I answered him: his challenge is from the outset for two reasons. First, certainly Tur holds like most Poskim from the reason lest one come to eat from it. However the penalty is not stricter than the prohibition itself: where the chametz itself on Pesach is forbidden in benefit — they penalized that also after Pesach it is forbidden in benefit like on Pesach itself; but where on Pesach itself it is permitted in benefit and only forbidden in eating — the penalty is not stricter than the prohibition itself, and especially for other chametz: just as they required destruction from lest one eat — so there is concern lest one benefit, since benefit is also forbidden from the Torah; therefore they again penalized in eating — a penalty for eating and in benefit a penalty for benefit, and each is forbidden for its own reason; but a thing that on Pesach itself is not forbidden in benefit, only in eating — the main prohibition of retaining is from the concern of eating; therefore it suffices to forbid it in eating, not in benefit — what act does benefit perform here. And further, in truth his intent is not from this that ta'aruvot are forbidden in retaining — one may say ta'aruvot are certainly permitted in retaining; only the chametz itself certainly required destruction and has no permission except through mixture, and behold he mixed it with his hands — one does not nullify a prohibition l'chatchila; and even though it is in its permitted time, nevertheless since chametz is its name upon it, it is sometimes called a forbidden mixture and sometimes a permitted mixture; therefore the Sages enacted that if he mixed with his hands, after Pesach its law is like what mixed by itself on Pesach — and behold if it mixed by itself it was in any case forbidden in eating on Pesach itself; let it be that after Pesach it would be permitted — nevertheless now that he mixed with his hands in order to retain it, we judge after Pesach like those that mixed by itself on Pesach regarding Pesach itself, and it is after Pesach as if today were Pesach; but for benefit, since if today were Pesach and it mixed by itself it would be permitted in benefit — retaining after he mixed with his hands is no worse than if it mixed by itself on Pesach regarding Pesach itself. And with this understand our words in Baer Heitev there well — examine; and see Magen Avraham note 9 regarding honey water and siman 447, etc. — all imply that lees in honey have the status of ma'amid upon it; but see Sefer Tuv Ta'am in Tractate Avodah Zarah 34 s.v. hani durdayei — it is clear there that he holds lees in honey water have no status of ma'amid at all, for it is only to be effervescent — see there where he elaborated; and one may join his view here when needed, and I have no leisure to elaborate — perhaps God will grant me another time to explore his words more — examine."
  },
  "output/siman_442/dagul-merevavah/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "And he holds: since burning was permitted for a need, it was also permitted not for a need; but to bake is forbidden, for even for a need we do not say since it is permitted for a need, etc. — see Rambam chapter 1 of Laws of Yom Tov; and Tur holds like the plain meaning of Rambam's words there that specifically for carrying out and burning we say \"from the need of\" and not for other melachot even for ochel nefesh itself — unlike the Rav Maggid there; and further even per Maggid Mishneh other melachot were not permitted — only one does not receive lashes from the reason \"since guests happen to arrive,\" as explained in Rambam there law 15; unlike carrying out and burning which were permitted from the Torah entirely even not for need at all. And what Taz wrote siman 446 note 3 in the name of Tur that even if they did not nullify it is forbidden to burn on Yom Tov — Taz cited first Tur's words who wrote thus in the name of \"some say,\" but in the conclusion Tur concludes in the name of his brother R' Yechiel that one may not burn on Yom Tov if they did not nullify; and likewise nokshah — this cannot be nullified even while still nokshah, for it is forbidden in benefit d'rabbanan and not in his domain to nullify it.",
    "2:_": "And likewise it is proven from Tosafos Pesachim 29b s.v. Rav Ashi at the end of the discussion: otherwise how did they prove from \"from the time of chametz\" that to destroy them one does not transgress — behold there is me'ilah on the value of a perutah and bal yera'eh on a kezayit; and perhaps it deals where there is not a kezayit but there is the value of a perutah — rather certainly that also for bal yera'eh half a measure is forbidden from the Torah; and see Tractate Shevuot 16b in Tosafos s.v. mah tamei and examine, for it implies we do not say regarding what is not a prohibition of eating that half a measure is forbidden from the Torah; and perhaps for bal yera'eh, since it is a food matter, we say as for eating half a measure is forbidden from the Torah, so too for bal yera'eh; and what appears more correct to me: in a matter that is not a prohibition of eating, half a measure is not forbidden from the Torah but forbidden d'rabbanan — if so Tosafos' words in Shevuot 16b fit properly, and also Tosafos' words in Pesachim 29b are correct that also what is forbidden d'rabbanan again has no me'ilah, for they wrote there that this which one does not redeem is only d'rabbanan and he does not commit me'ilah."
  },
  "output/siman_442/eshel-avraham/part-001.txt": {
    "2:_": "That putrefied. From this Eshel Avraham forbids his questioner with recurring headache to leave a kilyor of chametz and spices on his temples even if less than kezayit within k'dei achilat peras.",
    "4:_": "To eat it. Even per those who permit in 447 — here agree lest chametz inside establishes it — Taz; Chayei Adam: no doubt his doubt removes Ra'avad's certainty.",
    "5:_": "To destroy them. See Baer Heitev s.k. 11 in Maharam of Lublin. Taz challenged Yoreh De'ah 87: cooked in stomachs permitted in sixty — likewise here nullify kezayit for se'or has power in ma'amid to give taste on Pesach; Rashal holds davar hama'amid permitted to retain; Rashba and great Poskim forbid; therefore Ramban forbids cooking mei einayim cooked all year when first was sour with beer lees — this fourth or fifth — Ra'avan 72b.",
    "7:_": "Nullified when not in kneading place. Peri Chadash; Rosh: did not elaborate these laws for Israel scrape all chametz even a trace; Taz challenged — we are obligated per Tosafos lest eat — his words strained.",
    "9:א": "Permitted. Per Maggid Mishneh likewise se'or; Ra'avad: different — fit to leaven; also designated for kneading or coated in clay — prohibition not nullified.",
    "9:ב": "To retain. D'oraisa permitted in eating — even on Yom Kippur exempt eating unfit food; only d'rabbanan decreed equating it to fit food — Rosh."
  },
  "output/siman_442/levushei-serad/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "There and it is nullified in sixty — end of his words. The words \"end of his words\" are out of place — should read \"beforehand\"; but for R' Eliezer forbidden — should read \"but for R' Eliezer therefore forbidden.\"",
    "2:_": "Magen Avraham s.k. 9: davar hama'amid even in thousand not nullified, etc. — see Machatzit HaShekel klal 79 s.k. 3.",
    "3:_": "Taz s.k. 4: some lenient to drink on last Yom Tov mei einayim, etc. — see Kenesset Yisrael 87:14."
  },
  "output/siman_442/netiv-chayim/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "(Magen Avraham s.k. 1) Since it began to leaven therefore burn — note: meaning burn afterward when it became chametz as Bach ruled full chametz made on Yom Tov destroyed on Yom Tov — see below 446 s.k. 1. Rashba holds like other Poskim who forbid burning on Yom Tov — must explain Rabbanan did not distinguish.",
    "2:_": "(Taz s.k. 1) And proof for this. Note: Tur's language should be corrected — certainly regarding mixtures he wrote only no lav in mixtures — implying d'oraisa prohibition exists; what he wrote \"only rabbinic prohibition\" follows on nokshah; if we derive from everything — nokshah too should be rabbinic; one may say reasonable to establish mixtures as prohibition since b'eyna d'oraisa forbidden — half measure applies.",
    "3:_": "(Magen Avraham s.k. 14) And wonder on Tur. Note: to me the key: Tur forbids eating because he designates it even for one who does not hold that view — nevertheless forbidden in eating lest not charred well; but regarding mixture he follows his conclusion 447 end permitting to mix l'chatchila to retain — likewise 442 in Rif's method; this is the \"some say\" Rosh brought — however Magen Avraham strained to learn how forbidden in benefit is proven from not writing permitted in eating — appears first view: morayis permitted in eating because full chametz through mixture permitted in benefit after Pesach and charring before its time even b'eyna permitted in benefit and charring through mixture adds one lenient level permitted even in eating — disagrees — if so greater novelty charring before its time permitted in eating; therefore Tur's words are correct."
  },
  "output/siman_442/rabbi-akiva-eiger/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "Magen Avraham s.k. 7: not more advantageous than moldy bread; Makor Chayim wondered — moldy bread deals with disqualified from dog; he wrote should read not more than nokshah through mixture — see there.",
    "2:_": "Seif 7: if kezayit in one place; if reduced from kezayit — Yerushalmi asks if nullified or since visible must destroy all.",
    "3:_": "Seif 8: whatever they asked — remove thread; even if dry — by removing not taken; Gemara challenges: if moist would peel all as one — even dry obligated to destroy — Yerushalmi."
  },
  "output/siman_442/shaarei-teshuvah/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "They transgress. Baer Heitev; see Magen Avraham's challenge from se'or must burn; Chidushei E.H.; Makor Chayim; Keter Kehunah; Beit Efrayim 30 to establish Magen Avraham; Penei Yehoshua 15: one forgot to destroy candied sugar coating of nuts and fruits within Pesach — craftsmen say they cook sugar in water and coat — flour needed — possibly 60 with water; only chametz nokshah; agreed chametz nokshah may retain — see there; I have doubt perhaps flour leavened before sugar melted and water causes leavening without syrup mixture — if sugar already cooked in water then flour added perhaps like raisin wine siman 462; Chacham Tzvi disagrees there — not 60; Acharonim nokshah requires destruction d'rabbanan — if remembered within Pesach must destroy — no leniency to retain; if craftsmen put flour only when water boils well — slight leniency from chalitah — see below 434 s.k. 3; Chayei Adam: no absorbed-vessel prohibition on Pesach — Har HaCarmel 14 permits retaining preserves or fat in chametz pots — see there.",
    "3:_": "And all the like. Baer Heitev; Chacham Tzvi 20: burnt wine from malts forbidden d'oraisa — though taste changed essence is malts for burnt wine; like very rotten fruit unfit alive eaten by cooking in water or honey and sugar — though taste changed in cooking they remain; see there; Penei Yehoshua 101 for kiddushin elaborated Shevut Yaakov 10:191; Beit Efrayim 49 responsum to Gaon R' Yehoshua on preserving burnt wine as mere zei'ah; Be'er Yaakov debated with colleagues — see responsa Mishna Berurah 64 — seemed unwilling to fix halachah to permit from zei'ah even with nullification; Beit Efrach what Gaon wrote in name of author of Ahavat Chametz on malts sale — malts is chametz nokshah — see there; thus Chacham Tzvi's opening implication d'oraisa transgression ben yomo not necessary — all per many Poskim nokshah rabbinic; nevertheless there I explained all per circumstance if spoiled until entirely unfit except b'dieved — Chacham Tzvi may deal with malts whose taste changed yet still somewhat fit after grinding — not implied from rotten fruit comparison — requires study.",
    "7:_": "Visible. Baer Heitev; Gilyon Maharsha klal 4 siman 11: custom on hats covering garment inside — coat with soft batter near two eggs, attach outer garment, sew, place on heat until dry as hat — no chametz concern though dough reached se'or category since action done and nullified and covered with stitches — see there; responsa Rav Zuskind Rothenberg (questioner cited Taz siman 432) resolves: do not challenge from hiding — here not visible outside nullification helps; Terumat HaDeshen: reason not visible outside is chametz form nullified — see there.",
    "11:_": "In vinegar. Baer Heitev; Penei Me'irot 107 wondered on Magen Avraham s.k. 9 in Ra'avan (in Penei Me'irot and Sha'arei Yosef copied Ramban — error) — even fifth honey leavened in beer lees forbidden; Yoreh De'ah 112 and 115 in R' Yehuda permit after three times; Penei Me'irot 2:75 also on Magen Avraham — wrote thus to permit one careful about chadash (I lacked heart to permit retaining on Pesach; retaining appears permitted — see there; Sha'arei Yosef 2:77 rejected — they lenient only in their bread and milk which are light prohibitions — not chametz which is stringent — therefore stringent even in retaining; if transgressed and retained may permit benefit after Pesach combining reason some Poskim write that now all nullify — do not be so stringent — see there; see below 447 s.k. 14 on Baer Heitev from Bach responsum; see 448 regarding beer lees on eve of Pesach."
  },
  "output/siman_442/ateret-zekenim/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "A chametz mixture — only when there is a kezayit of chametz within k'dei achilat peras do one transgress bal yera'eh and bal yimatzei (Gemara chapter Elu Overin, Maharam).",
    "5:_": "Beer made from wheat and barley, etc., because there is a kezayit within k'dei achilat peras and more (Maharam). There was an incident in Worms: a Jew cooked honey and leavened it with honey lees he had cooked all year; the first honey had soured with beer lees, and what he cooked now was the fourth batch. Rabbenu Yaakov ben R' Yitzchak instructed him to leave it until after Pesach and sell it; the sages of his generation disagreed. His reason: R' Shimon does not penalize where it is not b'eyna. He wrote it is astonishing in his eyes — a davar hama'amid is as if it is b'eyna, and if so the lees that leavened are also as if b'eyna — end of his words. Per my explanation there is nothing astonishing: those beer lees placed in honey water are not for taste; they are not put in for taste but so it will ferment and rise quickly, as Tosafos Avodah Zarah wrote. Also what the Gemara says — this vinegar of beer of gentiles is entirely forbidden — therefore it is not considered as b'eyna and is nullified in sixty. However honey water into which beer lees themselves are placed — it appears one must destroy it, not because of taste but because the lees sink to the bottom of the barrel and do not mix into the honey water; therefore the lees are considered as b'eyna, give taste during Pesach, and are not nullified. And even though they mix below within the lees — nevertheless per what I wrote below on folio 131a that beer is nullified even within Pesach in sixty, it is plainly permitted at least to sell — investigate.",
    "7:_": "Dough in cracks, etc. — only when one does not knead in that kneading-trough all year is it nullified in its minority; but if one kneads there all year, even washing in hot water and scraping do not help — one must give it as a gift to a gentile or coat it with clay — end of his words. {Rama: in the name of Maharansh.} He wondered: what of it if the stomach fell from above into a chametz vessel — wine is not kavush until after above, and after above the vessel becomes damaged; see Yoreh De'ah siman 105 seif 1 and Shach s.k. 2 who elaborated to permit — end of his words. I was awestruck at the above Rav's conduct — it was not proper or lawful to reply to the Ari z\"l alive after his death and overturn Maharach's words, for he was great in wisdom and pilpul and all later Poskim were fluent from his mouth — did he not also see the Acharonim in siman 105 cited? On the contrary, in waiving his honor he erred in proof — Shulchan Aruch Yoreh De'ah siman 437 seif 1 regarding yayin nesech, see there it is explicit like Maharach; also the practical halachah escaped him that Baal Beit Hilul ruled siman 69 and siman 105 not like those Acharonim — see there; also he did not see Chok Yosef in Chelek Chokhmat Da'at letter 20 in the name of the Gaon R' Yaakov of Lublin (Maharach) that he heard in his name there is a distinction if the vessel stands empty then the vessel becomes damaged — and now he struck Maharach on the head in vain in his astonishment — end of his words. In this siman Magen David s.k. 5: therefore all those obligations are only for lashes, etc. — if so why did Tur and Shulchan Aruch bring these laws when we have no practical nafka mina from this? One must say there is a great nafka mina even now regarding kiddushin of a woman — if forbidden d'oraisa and liable to lashes for them she is not betrothed — and other distinctions — end of his words."
  },
  "output/siman_442/yad-ephraim/part-001.txt": {
    "1:_": "There in Magen Avraham s.k. 8: per Tur se'or must burn — Acharonim already challenged: on Yom Tov forbidden to burn; they resolved Tur follows his brother R' Yechiel 446 without nullification forbidden; here se'or cannot be nullified forbidden in benefit not in his domain to nullify. In my opinion: per Tosafos Pesachim 5 — though may benefit from ash, since cannot benefit at start of destruction forbidden; at start of destruction could benefit if leaves more wood — this and that cause; but Magen Avraham 445: even zuz gavra forbidden in chametz; therefore here while still se'or and nokshah — nokshah nullified in 60 Pesachim 105b — nevertheless if establishing cause is d'oraisa prohibition not so stringent in benefit; also mishnah se'or burn may be like Chachamim who permit even facing it without permitting cause — permitted even at start of destruction for nokshah nullified in 60; even per Shach ash forbidden — was bread's enhancement; for Chachamim who permit facing fire since flame from coal and coal forbidden for them — nevertheless mishnah se'or burn is like R' Yehuda chametz by burning; mishnah teaches one who eats exempt; which is se'or like grasshopper horns — see there — R' Yehuda; if so may burn and benefit; Tosafos s.v. bashelah on coals — distinction coals l'chatchila forbidden; all the more facing fire for Rabbanim forbidden l'chatchila; here se'or itself only rabbinically forbidden in eating — permitted in this case l'chatchila — all the more with permitting cause.",
    "2:_": "There deals if kezayit within k'dei achilat peras, etc. — law not precise; truly Gemara establishes Rabbanan argue R' Eliezer on paste because no k'dei achilat peras — Magen Avraham means there proved when no k'dei achilat peras permitted to Rabbanan though essence so; he resolved: Rashba only where essence so rabbinically forbidden; Gemara there d'oraisa; requires study — Gemara where Rabbanan argue is for eating and if no k'dei achilat peras eating not combined; not like Rashba on retaining — if his intent from why Rabbanan argue on paste — Gemara intended per R' Nachman mishnah like R' Eliezer and Rabbanan argue on mixture; therefore Magen Avraham challenged from kezayit — even without kezayit since essence so; should have challenged plainly on establishment that mishnah even like Rabbanan agree when essence so; Gemara requires study; Magen Avraham and R' Yehuda wrote: but mixed intentionally — meaning fixed method is so — not nullified in 60 even before Pesach — like Rashba; there implies even not initially intentional to retain — forbidden since essence is through chametz.",
    "3:_": "Taz s.k. 4: already practiced to beware chametz mei einayim of Pesach in lees of mei einayim or those vessels — should read thus. Established in vessel, etc."
  }
};
